    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "8a859fff-d31b-38be-99fb-af3f4e80a351",
                "word": "inkhorn",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "f597e124-07ad-432f-8c11-e02def27303a",
                        "entry_id": "8a859fff-d31b-38be-99fb-af3f4e80a351",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "inkhorns"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "95d310a5-17f1-3b4f-b2ce-f8ed41662b16",
                        "entry_id": "8a859fff-d31b-38be-99fb-af3f4e80a351",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A small portable container, often made of horn, used to carry ink."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "archaic"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c9eff66a-53bd-4605-845b-72b570fce777",
                                "sense_id": "95d310a5-17f1-3b4f-b2ce-f8ed41662b16",
                                "text": "Goe good partner, goe get you to Francis Seacole, bid him bring his pen and inkehorne to the Gaole: we are now to examine thoſe men."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f7e86a40-600d-463b-8cb3-2d33b02c1227",
                                "sense_id": "95d310a5-17f1-3b4f-b2ce-f8ed41662b16",
                                "text": "[…]and one man among them was clothed with linnen, with a writers inkehorne by his side, and they went in and stood beside the brasen altar."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e5659a28-118b-430f-a740-ef3689e51070",
                                "sense_id": "95d310a5-17f1-3b4f-b2ce-f8ed41662b16",
                                "text": "Men in helmets have divided that, with swords; men in wigs, with quill and inkhorn, to divide it: and even more hateful these latter, if more peaceably; for the wig-method is at once irresistibler and baser."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f98e7ba6-f3b6-4d31-bbae-d265ca6d1ad1",
                                "sense_id": "95d310a5-17f1-3b4f-b2ce-f8ed41662b16",
                                "text": "[…] from his pocket the notary drew his papers and inkhorn,\nWrote with a steady hand the date and the age of the parties,"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a013fb69-847d-454a-b003-83231e2ecca5",
                                "sense_id": "95d310a5-17f1-3b4f-b2ce-f8ed41662b16",
                                "text": "“All right,” said Haley, his face beaming with delight; and pulling out an old inkhorn, he proceeded to fill out a bill of sale, which, in a few moments, he handed to the young man."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "02056cf6-1103-47b0-b8d8-47e9d7330bd7",
                                "sense_id": "95d310a5-17f1-3b4f-b2ce-f8ed41662b16",
                                "text": "When a man of the theatre stirs his inkhorn he is frequently tempted to write a play about the theatre."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "5708d20b-6d96-3547-9bd2-2b0b0735fe12",
                        "entry_id": "8a859fff-d31b-38be-99fb-af3f4e80a351",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Something or someone pedantic, obscurely scholarly."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "attributive",
                            "derogatory"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2fc99ee3-bde5-476d-8ab3-d3dd32d44c60",
                                "sense_id": "5708d20b-6d96-3547-9bd2-2b0b0735fe12",
                                "text": "And ere that we will ſuffer ſuch a Prince,\nSo kinde a Father of the Common-weale,\nTo be diſgraced by an Inke-horne Mate,\nWee and our Wiues and Children all will fight\nAnd haue our bodyes ſlaughtred by thy foes."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9aab0b09-2e4a-47ac-9d66-391df026a098",
                                "sense_id": "5708d20b-6d96-3547-9bd2-2b0b0735fe12",
                                "text": "The tale retained its bookish and even inkhorn appeal, since established short-story forms continued to be read, and new ones written."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
